From 92de91bc3aff08b56a93cd40b29f074855f11f73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:25:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 25/29] Software mode: the guest is not given a GPU, and
 nothing in the session asks for one

Not a smaller session - a different machine, so the stack above the driver (native ARM64 Steam, FEX,
Proton, DXVK, gamescope) can be exercised while Mali is not in the picture. That is the only way left
to answer "is the stack sound?" without another reboot riding on the answer.

Enforcement is where the guest is built, not in an env var, because a bind mount is a fact and an
environment variable is a request:

  - LinuxRuntime.binds() skips bindGpuNode() entirely, so the fake /dev/dri/renderD128 and the
    /sys/dev/char stand-in are never made. That is the half that matters.
  - /dev/mali0 and /dev/kgsl-3d0 are then shadowed with the runtime's own zero-length file, on the
    precedent this class has used for years to hide /sys/fs/selinux.
  - /dev/null is NOT used, and that is the interesting bit: /dev/null is a character device, so
    stat() looks like a GPU node, S_ISCHR passes, and our ICD adapter would advertise major 1 /
    minor 3 to gamescope as a perfectly good render node. A regular file fails that probe, which is
    the code path the driver package already handles and the test suite already covers.
  - droiddeck-session makes the choice final: VK_DRIVER_FILES is set to the imported software
    manifest (replacing the loader's whole search, which is what keeps the runtime's PanVK out), and
    a missing or unreadable one is a refusal (DD_SOFTWARE_DRIVER_MISSING, exit 78) rather than the
    existing "IGNORING ...; using the runtime's own" fallback. Falling back is how a no-GPU session
    ends up opening one.
  - the pre-flight probe does not run, and says so in the log and the report: its whole job is
    submitting a job to kbase and waiting on its fence. droiddeck-mali-check is gated the same way,
    with BL_SOFTWARE_VULKAN != 1 on both Mali checks so the refusal message cannot end up telling a
    software session to import a hardware driver.
  - LinuxVulkanDriver.driverForSession() overrides the stored choice: on a Mali device the runtime's
    own driver IS the hardware driver, so "no choice" is a choice.

LinuxVulkanDriverManager grew companion libraries (next commit's zip needs libLLVM beside
libvulkan_lvp.so) and a vendor label; SafeProfile grew [SOFTWARE] and [avoidsGpu], the panel grew the
fourth choice with a note about what it does and what it costs, and the report now states the mode -
so a watchdog sample in software mode is read as what it is.

Checked here: bash -n on the session script passes (the pre-existing sh -n failure at line 25 is the
script's process substitution, and the committed version fails it identically - so it is the script's
dialect, not this change), both strings files parse as XML with no bare apostrophes for aapt2, and
SafeProfile's four entries each set all fourteen constructor parameters. NOT checked: compilation and
the runtime behaviour - no JDK or device here. The enum-count check exists because this same file
failed CI the last time an entry was left half-written.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
